<item>
            <title>[LU-20886] immediate send reads FastReg-mapped payload with local_dma_lkey</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20886</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;Description&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since &lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-14008&quot; title=&quot;avoid data copy with immediate message sending.&quot; class=&quot;issue-link&quot; data-issue-key=&quot;LU-14008&quot;&gt;&lt;del&gt;LU-14008&lt;/del&gt;&lt;/a&gt; an immediate message sends its payload by reference. kiblnd_fmr_map_tx() registers the payload pages with a FastReg MR and collapses them into one fragment (first page&apos;s DMA address, full length, MR key). kiblnd_init_tx_sge() then uses local_dma_lkey for that fragment instead of rd_key, so the HCA reads physical memory linearly from the first page. Any payload that crosses a page boundary into a non-contiguous page sends the wrong bytes for the second page. The sender reports success.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ptlrpc buffers are kmalloc&apos;d and physically contiguous, so this is latent there unless the buffer fell back to vmalloc. It is immediately visible with non-contiguous pages, e.g. lnet-selftest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&#160;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Impact on Lustre I/O&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On 64K-page clients &lt;b&gt;short I/O is always off&lt;/b&gt; (OST_MAX_SHORT_IO_BYTES is 0 there, osc.*.short_io_bytes reads 0 and cannot be raised), so every small write is a bulk. When the OSC gets -EDQUOT (project quota near its hard limit), vvp_io_commit_sync() clips the queued pages to the written range, and a small write crossing a page boundary becomes one bulk of two partial pages. The client&apos;s REPLY to the OST&apos;s LNetGet() takes the immediate send path, and the bytes after the page boundary are replaced by whatever physically follows the first page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The OST reports &quot;BAD WRITE CHECKSUM&quot; on extents of a few hundred bytes crossing a 64K boundary; the client&apos;s recomputed checksum matches its original, fabric counters stay clean, and the client ends with &quot;too many resent retries&quot;. Reads are unaffected (RDMA path). Reproduced with a 64K-page client over FastReg o2ib, a project quota set just above current usage, and a non-root process appending log lines in that project. A 4K-page client does not reproduce (short I/O keeps these writes out of bulk).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&#160;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
